'''arming-buckle†''' (är′ming-buk″l), ''n.'' '''1.''' A buckle used in defensive armor.—'''2.''' In ''her.'', such a
[[FIGURE]]
{{c|{{smaller|Four Varieties of Heraldic Arming-Buckles.}}}}
buckle, generally having the shape of a lozenge, used as a bearing.

'''arming-doublet†''' (är′ming-dub″let), ''n.'' A doublet used in military dress, forming an essential part of the harness of a light-armed foot-soldier in the middle ages and later. {{smaller|It is probable that it was a very close-fitting garment worn under the corselet. The general use of it seems to have suggested a style or cut in elegant costume, as we read of arming-doublets of costly material.}}
{{smaller block|That every man have an ''arming-doublet'' of fustian or canvas. {{right|''Duke of Norfolk’s Orders'', Hen. VIII., an. 36.}}}}
{{smaller block|An ''arming-doublet'' of crimson and yellow satin . . . with threads of Venice gold.
{{right|''Inventory Henry VIII.'', 1542, quoted by Planché.}}}}

'''arming-point†''' (är′ming-point), ''n.'' A point used in fastening together parts of a suit of armor.

