'''arming-press''' (är′ming-pres), ''n.'' A small hand-power stamping-press used by bookbinders. {{smaller|Its earliest employment was in stamping heraldic arms on the sides of books, whence its name. In the United States this form of press is known as a ''stamping-press'' or ''embossing-press.''}}

'''arming-spear†''' (är′ming-spēr), ''n.'' A spear used in war.

'''arming-sword†''' (är′ming-sōrd), ''n.'' A sword made especially for use in battle, as distinguished from one worn as a part of military dress or uniform in time of peace, and from that used in tourneys or the like.
{{smaller block|<poem>A helmett of proofe shee strait did provide,
A strong ''arming-sword'' shee girt by her side.</poem>
{{right|''Percy’s Reliques.''}}}}

'''Arminian''' (är-min′i-an), ''n.'' and ''a.'' [< NL. ''Arminianus'', < ''Arminius'', Latinized from the name of ''Harmensen.''] '''I.''' ''n.'' '''1.''' One of a Christian sect named from James Arminius (Jacobus Harmensen), a Protestant divine of Leyden, Netherlands (1560–1609). {{smaller|Its members were also called ''Remonstrants'', from a statement of their views in the form of a remonstrance presented to the States-General in 1610. They separated from the Calvinists, objecting to their doctrine of predestination. The sect as a distinct organization is chiefly confined to the Netherlands, where it numbers only about twenty congregations and a few thousand adherents.}}

